POINT 10 (live region + focus restoration) — FAIL.
Rendered GET /accounts: the only stable, pre-existing aria-live region
outside the HTMX swap target is #accounts-error-region (sr-only, lives in
accounts-content, outside #accounts-list). The pattern-overlap block's new
role="status"/aria-live="polite" lives entirely INSIDE
accounts-list-partial, i.e. inside the fragment #accounts-list replaces via
hx-swap="innerHTML" on every mutation.
Proved this mechanically: tagged the warning node with a probe attribute,
submitted the acct-a edit form (a real state-changing swap), and re-queried
after the swap. Result: WARNING_NODE_IS_SAME_AFTER_SWAP = false — the node
is destroyed and a brand-new element is inserted with role/aria-live already
baked onto it at insertion time, not an existing registered live region
whose text content changed. This is exactly the failure mode point 10 (and
the task brief) describes: several screen readers (NVDA, VoiceOver
historically) only announce mutations to a live region that was already
present and registered before the change; a region that is born with its
final content in one paint is frequently not announced at all. The
worker's own comment defends this by analogy to the visible error block's
role="alert" — but that analogy argues against the change: the codebase
already has the *correct* pattern for this exact problem
(#accounts-error-region, a stable node outside the swap target whose text
is what changes), and the warning block does not use it.
Concrete fix: add a stable, always-rendered (not `{{if .Warnings}}`-gated)
sr-only live-region container in accounts-content, outside #accounts-list,
mirroring #accounts-error-region, and make every response (full page and
the accounts-list-partial swap) populate its text from .Warnings — e.g. via
hx-swap-oob on that node in the partial, the same way #accounts-error-region
would need updating to survive a swap. role="status" on the visible amber
box can stay for redundancy, but it cannot be the only mechanism.

Focus restoration: submitted the acct-a edit form (focused its "Save
changes" button first) and read document.activeElement after the swap.
Focus landed on #acct-new-id (the "Account ID" field in the unrelated "Add
an account" form), not on anything related to acct-a or the new warning.
Root cause: `data-focus-target="id"` on that field
(web/templates/pages/accounts.html, "Add an account" section) is
unconditional — unlike every other data-focus-target in the template, which
is gated by `{{if eq .ErrorField "..."}}`. htmx:afterSwap's handler always
finds it first via `target.querySelector('[data-focus-target]')` and steals
focus regardless of what actually happened. This bug is NOT part of S4's
diff (git diff confirms handlers.go/accounts.html changes are scoped to the
Warnings plumbing and the two doc-comment blocks; this input's markup is
untouched) — recorded as an OBSERVATION, not charged to this task — but it
does mean the task brief's specific question ("check where focus lands")
has an answer worth flagging: focus restoration for this exact interaction
is currently broken for reasons S4 did not introduce and did not fix
either.

POINT 14 (banners dismissible / re-announced only on state change) — FAIL.
Template has no dismiss control anywhere in the warning block (grepped for
dismiss/localStorage/sessionStorage in accounts.html: none). Loaded
GET /accounts three times in a row with the overlap condition unchanged:
the block rendered identically every time (`grep -c "Pattern overlap
warning"` = 1, 1, 1) with no session-level suppression. Point 14's text is
explicit: "dismissible via keyboard and re-announced only on state change,
not every page load within a session." A pattern-overlap warning is a
banner by the constitution's own parenthetical ("unassigned files,
staleness warnings" — this is the same class of advisory persistent-state
banner). As built it has neither property: not dismissible, and it renders
(and, if the live-region worked, would re-announce) on every load and every
partial swap while the condition persists, not just once per
state-change/session. This is not softened by the warning being useful —
point 14's text does not carve out an exception for "important" banners.

POINT 8 (not color alone) — PASS.
Rendered DOM confirms: amber background + `<svg aria-hidden="true">`
triangle-warning icon + literal visible text "Pattern overlap warning"
(span.font-medium). Meaning is carried by icon and text, not amber alone.

POINT 7 / 12 (contrast, both themes) — FAIL (light theme only).
